{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "IT Solutions for Your Enterprise."}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear or use to talk a solution provider."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Make the result report from the analysis of the information system, based on the customer requirements, following established rules and protocols"}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Participates in simple conversations or simulated situations about the communicative topics for this level through the appropriate use of simple pronunciation aspects, and grammar and vocabulary structures. Recognizes simple ideas on requirement reports, tools for Object Oriented Programming, and other basic programming tools."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob01re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Modal Verbs Review, Have To, Can"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "This learning point helps you to understand the use of the modal verbs Can and Have to."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><strong>CAN</strong></p> <p><strong>Can</strong> is very easy to use because you use it with any subject pronoun and its form never changes. Moreover, when you combine <strong>can</strong> with a verb, it is not necessary to add &ldquo;s&rdquo; to the end of that verb.</p> <p>You use the modal verb <strong>can</strong> with different purposes; for example, with it, it is possible to talk about abilities and possibilities. Also, you use this modal verb to make requests.</p> <p>Take a look at how you use <strong>can</strong> in the affirmative, negative, and interrogative forms.</p> <p>Affirmative</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>S + can + verb base form + complement</strong></p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I can fix a computer.</i></p> <p>Negative</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>S + can&rsquo;t (cannot) + verb base form + complement</strong></p> <p><i>We can&rsquo;t improve your software right now.</i></p> <p>Interrogative</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Can + s + verb base form + complement?</strong></p> <p><i>Can you help us with the programming?</i></p> <p><strong>HAVE TO</strong></p> <p>When you use <strong>have to, especially</strong> in the affirmative form, you want to refer to actions that are mandatory to do. In other words, <strong>have to</strong> helps you to talk about obligations.</p> <p>When you use have to in the negative form, the meaning changes a little bit. <strong>Have&nbsp;</strong><strong>to</strong> in the negative form means that some action is not necessary to do.</p> <p>When you use <strong>have&nbsp;</strong><strong>to</strong>, you need to be careful because it changes depending on the subject pronoun (I, you, he, she, etc.) that you use. Take a look at the following chart. It provides you with the structures of have to in the affirmative, negative, and interrogative forms in the simple present tense.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<table style=\"border-color: black;\"> <tbody> <tr> <td colspan=\"4\" width=\"443\"> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>AFFIRMATIVE FORM</strong></p> </td> </tr> <tr> <td width=\"128\"> <p><strong>I, you, we, they</strong></p> </td> <td width=\"93\"> <p><strong>have to</strong></p> </td> <td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"111\"> <p><strong>upgrade</strong></p> </td> <td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"111\"> <p><strong>the PC&rsquo;s system.</strong></p> </td> </tr> <tr> <td width=\"128\"> <p><strong>he, she, it</strong></p> </td> <td width=\"93\"> <p><strong>has to</strong></p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<table style=\"border-color: black;\"> <tbody> <tr> <td colspan=\"4\" width=\"443\"> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>NEGATIVE FORM</strong></p> </td> </tr> <tr> <td width=\"128\"> <p><strong>I, you, we, they</strong></p> </td> <td width=\"123\"> <p><strong>don't have to</strong></p> </td> <td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"81\"> <p><strong>upgrade</strong></p> </td> <td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"111\"> <p><strong>the PC&rsquo;s system.</strong></p> </td> </tr> <tr> <td width=\"128\"> <p><strong>he, she, it</strong></p> </td> <td width=\"123\"> <p><strong>doesn't have to</strong></p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Structures:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<table style=\"border-color: black;\"> <tbody> <tr> <td width=\"221\"> <p><strong><i>Can</i></strong></p> </td> <td width=\"221\"> <p><strong><i>Have to</i></strong></p> </td> </tr> <tr> <td width=\"221\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><i>&nbsp;</i></p> <p><i>&nbsp;</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </td> <td width=\"221\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Affirmative:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>S + have to/ has to +&nbsp; verb base form + complement </strong></p> <p><strong><i>&nbsp;</i></strong></p> <p>I have to fix my brother&rsquo;s laptop I broke it.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </td> </tr> <tr> <td width=\"221\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> </td> <td width=\"221\"> <p>Negative:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>S +</strong> <strong>don&rsquo;t have to / doesn&rsquo;t have to</strong> <strong>+&nbsp; verb base form + complement </strong></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>You don&rsquo;t have to improve our software now, you can do it next week.</p> </td> </tr> <tr> <td width=\"221\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> </td> <td width=\"221\"> <p>Questions:</p> <p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p> <p><strong>Aux do/does + s + have to + verb + complement?</strong></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Do you have to hire a consultant?</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Wh + aux do/does + s + have to + verb + complement?</strong></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>What do you have to do to make my software work?</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "You can implement a backup solution plan and adopt data disaster recovery procedure."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You have to do it as soon as possible to avoid having more problems in the near future."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Common IT Problems In a Small Business"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The IT department at a company plays an important role. Its performance reflects on the growth and achievement of goals. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>The following is a description of the most common IT problems a small business can have.&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Integration Issues</strong></p> <p>When a business implements new technology and it is not done effectively, then, it can become inefficient because programmers may have to enter information twice or information ends up stored in different places.</p> <p><strong>Backup and Disaster Recovery Challenges</strong></p> <p>Data disasters such as power outage, accidentally deleting files, or a server crash can result expensive. Therefore, every business should plan a backup to avoid these critical experiences.</p> <p><strong>The main causes aren't uncovered or addressed-</strong></p> <p>Pay attention to small signs or problems; an issue can be the cause of a bigger problem. IT departments don&rsquo;t solve issues; they need to solve the root problem.</p> <p><strong>Security Risks</strong></p> <p>Prevent your system from being hacked. It may result more cost effective to consider investing on your business&rsquo; security. It may also keep a company out of trouble depending on how much sensitive information it keeps.</p> <p><strong>Waiting Until Something Breaks</strong></p> <p>Make sure you offer maintenance permanently to ensure all your system works properly. Every problem starts with a little signal which means many problems can be prevented and solved when the problem is still cheaper and easier to solve; don&rsquo;t wait until the problem may become urgent.</p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Waiting until something breaks and not being cautious about security risks, hackers can be targeting your company."}, {"type": "example", "content": "One enterprise had integration issues when new technologies were implemented, so every time they had to look for the information in different databases and information was duplicated."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "IT"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "Information Technology; a department of a company that handles computers, internet services, technology, and data systems"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We are planning to discuss  IT solutions for an enterprise."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob01aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "device"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a machine or equipment "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: An IT solution provider can recommend a new server, or provide maintenance to repair computers or any other device. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob01aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "backup "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make a copy of information in a computer to keep it safe in case it is lost"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can implement a backup solution plan and adopt data disaster recovery procedure."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob01aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "power outage "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an interruption in the supply of electricity"}, {"type": "part_speech", 